――An escape from the Lugunica royal castle, where they had spent two whole months under house arrest.

The breakout, which at one point had verged on sheer recklessness, had gone better than expected thanks to an unforeseen push from the outside. That said, internally, the potential addition of extra baggage and the eruption of a needless squabble meant the good and the bad balanced each other out.

At any rate—,

"Someone who's just hitchin' a ride like me's got no business bitchin' about it anyway."

That was the current self-assessment of Felt, who had been part of the castle escape party and considered herself to have played the smallest role among them.

The ones who had pulled off the heavy lifting to get them out of the castle were Ram, who had secretly coordinated with Marcos, Captain of the Guard; Filore, who had come to rescue Felt and the others in an unimaginable rampage; and, on top of that, Heinkel, acting as her minder.

Placed upon a complex game board and pulled apart by their differing positions and duties, Felt's camp had been in such a state that not only Felt, who had merely been confined, but even her knight Reinhard, along with her advisor Old Man Rom and Rachins and the boys, hadn't been able to participate in the escape.

Felt couldn't help but feel frustrated by the helplessness of her entire faction, but—,

"Still, Ram told me that because us candidates quietly went along with what they said, the castle's 'guard' ended up laxer than it would've been. If we hadn't, I don't think Heinkel-san would've been the only one hurt."

"Yes, exactly! I think so too! So stop sulking with that mopey face, Felt, and let's look forward! Think of Emilia and me as your big sisters and rely on us. ...Yes! As your sisters!"

With the two people in roughly the same boat as Felt carrying on like that, her gloomy mood was blown right away.

Putting Filore's nonsense aside, sinking into a funk just because you hadn't been useful in an escape that went well was far too much of an extravagant regret.

Extravagance was the enemy. For better or worse, extravagance was the sort of thing that put excess fat on both heart and body.

――Having weathered that shift in mindset, Felt and the others succeeded in escaping from the royal castle, and furthermore, slipped cleanly out of the Royal Capital as well. The short, swift maneuver of clearing the castle gates and melting into the common streets before the candidates' disappearance was noticed had paid off.

Originally, they had planned to get past the castle gates with the help of gate guards Marcos had arranged, but that had instead been accomplished through an alternate route Heinkel prepared: using a transport wagon for medical supplies.

"Even if they didn't know the full story, if it comes to light that soldiers were involved, they're likely to get grilled hard for it. If it's people outside the castle, they can just claim they were threatened and be done with it."

The one who had proposed that was Heinkel, battered and half-dead.

It made sense, but it also felt like he'd just wanted treatment for his own beat-up hide. Since they'd ultimately managed to break away from the Royal Capital, that was just Felt taking out her irritation—venting her pent-up frustration at having had no choice but to leave Reinhard behind at the castle.

Seeing Felt's complicated inner turmoil, Emilia knitted her strikingly shapely brows.

"You're worried about Reinhard, aren't you?"

"...Dunno. Rather'n bein' worried about the bastard, maybe I'm worried 'cause I got no clue what the bastard's gonna pull. Unlike bro, he ain't the fragile type, so he'll manage on his own just fine."

"Hehe, then what you're worried about is the same as me."

"The same? Me and Big Sis Emilia?"

"Mm, that's right. I'm sure Subaru's hale and hearty too. But no matter how much you tell him, he never fixes the way he pushes himself too hard and does reckless things, so that makes me reeally worried."

Facing Emilia, who smiled as if to say, See? The same, right?, Felt twisted her lips.

The awkward part was that she couldn't call it completely off the mark. Emilia actually had a surprisingly good head on her shoulders. Felt just wasn't good at handling it when someone laid things out with dead honesty.

If this hadn't been about Reinhard, she could have brushed it off a little more gracefully, but—,

"That idiot... no matter how much time passes, he never settles into a solid shape in my head..."

Hailed as the Kingdom's strongest and celebrated as the 'Knight of Knights', to hear Felt tell it, Reinhard was clueless about the world, even more clueless about himself, and a huge idiot to boot.

Despite having all that power, he couldn't keep a single, unshakeable core slammed right down the middle, so he was always wavering this way and that, his mind drifting and never settling down.

That was why, every single time, Felt had to give him a good smack on the back.

"――――"

Following that line of thought, Felt glanced briefly toward the door leading to the back room.

A few hours after leaving the Royal Capital, they were inside a safehouse hidden in a grove away from the highway. Having fled the city in the aforementioned transport wagon, the party had subsequently switched dragon carriages and decided to spend the night at this hideout they had reached.

In the back room right now, Filore was desperately treating the heavily injured Heinkel.

"Technically, using a sacrament without the Church's permission isn't proper, but then I thought of something. If I go about it as asking for forgiveness after the fact, I can use them as much as I want... I mean, using sacraments as the need arises must also be the will of the 'Divine Dragon', don't you think...?!"

Felt wondered if Filore clenching her fist in determination like that was permissible for a 'Saintess'.

Still, never having touched the doctrines of the 'Divine Dragon Church', and having no confidence in her ability to pass a fair judgment where Heinkel was concerned, Felt had no right to say anything. If she had to name one thing, once Heinkel could talk, she had something she wanted to verify regarding Reinhard, but that was about it.

So, whatever answer Filore brought back after shouting things like "Take that!" and "Nghhh!" beyond the door, her stance for now was to just watch and wait.

"For better or worse, Lady Filore and Heinkel making a move served as a distraction. The arrangements for passing the castle gates were theirs, while this safehouse was prepared on our end... With the origins mixed, it should confound any pursuers."

"What a relief. It's thanks to Filore and the others coming for us. But still, Marcos-san, did you really have to beat Heinkel-san into such a pulp...?"

"――. Given the circumstances, I must ask you to consider it unavoidable force majeure. I believe I would have held back more were my opponent anyone other than him."

"Meanin' you just put a little too much into it 'cause you didn't like the guy?"

"It means I knew he was a man who wouldn't stop unless taken that far. In fact, after all that, he didn't even pass out, let alone die. An Astrea through and through. ...Though I hardly need to tell you that, Lady Felt."

"Tch, poked the bush an' got a snake, huh."

With that, Felt clicked her tongue at Marcos, who had just returned after checking the perimeter around the hideout.

Back at the castle, he had worn a full suit of silver armor, but currently on the run, he had stripped off that conspicuous gear and changed into light, ordinary clothes no different from a common townsperson—and if anything, plain garments leaning closer to a resident of the slums. It suited him surprisingly well, but changing his clothes did nothing to hide his natural physique and imposing presence; it was a drop in the bucket.

Regardless, that attitude was proof that his cooperation wasn't a whim or a momentary lapse in judgment.

"Rock-face, what's our lead from here on out?"

"I have one, of course, but will you be accompanying us?"

"Makin' it this far just to say goodbye wouldn't make any sense. Besides, our goal's the same, ain't it? Beatin' the snot out of the bastards tryin' to trash the castle... nah, the Kingdom."

"Mm, that's right. Trashing the country is Felt-chan's campaign pledge, after all. I remember how it went right at the start, loud and clear."

"Hearin' that makes it sound like I'm the one pickin' a trashy fight."

Emilia had apparently intended to back her up, so she blinked in surprise at Felt's sour face. At their exchange, a faint, raspy breath slipped out.

Glancing over, it was Marcos, who seemed to have pressed the back of his hand to his mouth to stifle a laugh. At Felt's scowling glare, he added, "Pardon me," and continued,

"Your back-and-forth was so amusing, I couldn't help it. For competing royal candidates, you treat each other with quite a warm camaraderie, don't you?"

"Yes, exactly! We may be competing for the same throne, but we don't plan on glaring at each other with bared fangs. Right?"

"Ain't like we made a formal agreement or nothin', but yeah, that's how it is. It's not like we hate each other's guts, so snarlin' and snappin' like wild dogs'd just be stupid, wouldn't it?"

To the pacifistic Emilia's opinion, Felt replied with a dash of her own spice. Hearing both their answers, Marcos folded his thick arms and silently closed his eyes.

When he stood like that, he possessed a quiet presence that truly gave the illusion of a block of solid rock.

"...To tell the truth, when the holding of the Royal Selection became unavoidable, I braced myself. The battle for the throne among the royal candidates would shake the country to its core, and could even tear it apart. Amidst that, I spent my days asking myself what role we, the Royal Guard, ought to play."

Presently, the words the rock began to speak were a heartfelt plea from one who cared for the Kingdom from the bottom of his soul.

Indeed, it wasn't just Marcos; the 'Council of Wise Men' and those tied to the royal castle had undoubtedly been on pins and needles. After all, the Royal Selection was unprecedented on its own, yet the candidates who had gathered were all eccentrics—Felt and Emilia being the two foremost examples.

"I feel for ya. Must suck havin' to be Captain of the Guard at a crazy time like this."

"I appreciate your consideration. However, I hold a different view."

"A different view? Meanin'...?"

"I believe it was a blessing that you all were the candidates. Good, rational, and sincere. Which is precisely why I cannot help but regret failing to prevent the present state of affairs beforehand."

"...Well, the Kingdom bein' on the brink of collapse 'cause of the Sin Archbishops sure as hell ain't what anyone'd call healthy."

Felt figured half-hearted comfort was pointless against Marcos's deep sense of self-reproach.

The incompetence he felt was an undeniable fact. If the knights were the sword that protected the Kingdom, neither Marcos nor the order under his banner had lived up to that duty.

However, that just meant—,

"――We can't just keep taking this lying down. It's reeally frustrating, after all."

"Hah! When you're agreein' with Big Sis Emilia, it gets scary as hell."

"Huh, why?! I'd feel reeally reassured if you were on the same page, Felt-chan."

How unreasonable, Emilia seemed to say as her eyes widened, to which Felt merely gave a perfunctory shrug. At their exchange, Marcos laughed once more. But this time, it wasn't a quiet chuckle.

He opened his mouth and laughed out loud.

"Ha, hahaha! How amusing. You truly are an amusing bunch."

"Whoa, that surprised me."

"So that's what you're really like, Rock-face?"

At the two candidates' reactions, Marcos kept the lingering impulse of a grin on his curled lips.

"Ah, indeed. Being stiff doesn't suit my nature. In any case, by now, my authority as Captain of the Guard is frozen... or if they moved fast enough, I've likely been dismissed. Oh, I assure you, my respect remains intact."

"Mm, I can tell. Besides, you look a lot livelier this way, Marcos-san."

"Etiquette and manners are just like armor and helms. You should don them properly on the battlefields where they are needed, and when they aren't, taking them off and setting them aside is plenty. Provided you two permit it, of course."

"You heard Big Sis Emilia's answer, didn't ya? That's way easier for me to talk to, too. Bein' treated with all that respect's been givin' me the creeps from the start anyway."

In the first place, what they wanted to have from here on out was an open, honest talk. If that was the case, stripping away unnecessary formality made things far easier.

Receiving Felt and Emilia's answers, Marcos gave a deep nod.

"To return to the matter of our lead, I have a connection—one of the few in the Kingdom right now that can be trusted. This safehouse and the escape route from the castle were both arranged through that contact."

"You've got some buddies with pretty long reaches. Comrades who worry about the country's future, or somethin'?"

"I won't deny it. You may think of them as such."

"――――"

At Marcos's reply, Felt touched her canine tooth with a fingertip and narrowed her eyes.

Comrades who worried about the country's future—she had vaguely heard from Old Man Rom and Rachins that the Kingdom possessed some kind of defense organization outside the knights. Though from the slums, Felt had been born and raised in the Royal Capital, so she had always doubted whether such a thing actually existed.

"Seems like from the sound of it, they really do exist."

If the knights were the protectors of the light, was the organization in question the protectors of the shadows? A situation dire enough that they had to borrow the strength of the shadow protectors, who normally never surfaced.

I see, it really was an absolute crisis not just for the Royal Selection, but for the Kingdom of Lugunica itself.

"Is meeting up with those people our goal? Do you think they might know where Subaru and the others are... where everyone we can't reach went?"

"The probability is high. Far better than wandering aimlessly while harassed by pursuers, at the very least."

"――Presenting the worst-case scenario to make your preferred choice look better is a rather distasteful method, Lord Marcos."

Cutting in with that chilly remark was Ram, who had entered from a different door than the one leading to Filore's room.

Fixing Marcos with her light red eyes, she went on,

"Even without relying on such spiteful rhetoric, Lady Emilia is capable of making sound judgments. I would appreciate it if the Captain of the Royal Guard, of all people, refrained from resorting to such base tricks."

"Harsh. A trick learned from an ill-mannered old acquaintance... picked up straight from Roswaal, in fact."

"Any technique can be splendid or base depending on the person using it."

"Um, Ram, I feel like that sounded like a reeally selfish opinion just now... Ah, but I'm glad you believe in me. Thank you."

To Emilia, who had packed both the carrot and the stick into a single statement, Ram offered a light bow, saying, "Think nothing of it." Then, paying no mind to the wryly smiling Marcos, she turned back to Emilia and Felt.

"For now, the meal is ready. We can continue this after dinner."

"Really? Thank you. What's the 'menu'?"

"Steamed potatoes."

"Hehe, of course it is. It's reeally good luck."

Hearing Ram's crisp reply, Emilia accepted it in high spirits.

According to what Felt had heard, during the two months she had been under house arrest, Emilia had stubbornly insisted, "If I don't eat the steamed potatoes Ram makes once a day, there's no telling what I might do!", successfully keeping her attendant maid by her side.

Though Felt had had her own motives for wanting to keep Grassis glued to Reinhard, this was a move she never would have dreamed up, and she'd tipped her hat when she first heard it.

Regardless—,

"The sacrament treatment is done for now! I am completely, utterly, totally famished!"

As if she had timed it perfectly, Filore threw the door open with great vigor and appeared, bringing the night of their first day escaping the Royal Capital to a close alongside dinner.

――Essentially, the party's course of action was agreed upon without objection: they would follow Marcos's lead and aim to rendezvous with the 'protectors of the shadows'.

Filore and her group, who had originally intended to help Felt and the others escape, seemed to have had a plan of their own, but their scheme for after breaking out of the castle and the capital had been quite daring.

"I was planning to take Emilia and Felt and storm right into the Watergate City of Priestella! I thought maybe the three royal candidates could show up with a bam, save the people waiting for salvation, and slam an achievement the royal castle couldn't argue with right in their faces...!"

Had it been realized, it would have been quite a thrilling picture, but unfortunately, the rendezvous point with the 'protectors of the shadows' was said to be Milesta, a town in the eastern part of the Kingdom directly opposite Priestella.

Even so, while somewhat short-sighted, the strategy of intimidating the castle with an undeniable achievement wasn't a bad one. It might even be worth pulling off when an opening presented itself after meeting with the 'protectors of the shadows'.

If there was something she could do about the damage to Priestella, Felt wanted to do it too. If it made strategic sense as well, there was no reason not to consider it.

That said—,

"Countin' our chickens like that only works once we've turned over the cards we've got in our hands right now, though."

Fortunately, on the second and third days, no pursuers caught up with Felt's party during their flight.

This was thanks to the assistance of the 'protectors of the shadows' Marcos was in communication with, and likely also due to the effectiveness of the 'Cognitive Disruption Cloaks' he had prepared.

"I could only procure five cloaks including spares, so Heinkel, unable to walk on his own, has no choice but to blend in with the cargo. In practice, he truly is baggage, after all."

Such had been Marcos's remark, not softening his harshness toward Heinkel in the slightest.

Between his own natural toughness and Filore's desperate treatment with her sacraments, Heinkel had passed the critical stage. All that was left was for him to rest quietly, but in a life on the run, that was hardly something they could hope for.

For now, if they could reach their destination of Milesta without incident, the worries Felt and the others harbored, as well as Heinkel's condition, should clear up—or so it was supposed to be.

――Had they not been intercepted by pursuers on the Milesta Dragonback Bridge, right before their destination.

Construction of the Milesta Dragonback Bridge was said to have begun roughly three hundred years ago.

The Milesta Gorge, which severed the highway leading to the Royal Capital, had once been a troublesome bottleneck that gave travelers massive headaches. To cross to the opposite bank, one had to descend to the bottom of the valley and use a ferry depending on the river's mood, or spend days making a detour upstream.

To resolve that inconvenience, the Kingdom undertook the construction of a great bridge connecting both sides of the gorge.

The construction took a full twenty years. The completed bridge had an overall length of five hundred meters and reached a height of over a hundred meters from the water's surface, supported by numerous thick bridge piers built into the river, completing a wide roadway that wouldn't shake even under the traffic of dragon carriages.

In the morning, when the river mist filled the valley and hid the piers, only the roadway gently swelling toward the center appeared above the fog, resembling a colossal dragon lying across the valley. Thus, even before its completion, it had been affectionately called the 'Dragon's Back' among those involved in its construction.

Consequently, this colossal bridge came to be known as the 'Milesta Dragonback Bridge'. To make the name official, sculptures of a dragon's head and tail were placed at both ends of the bridge, and touching the tip of the snout of the dragon facing the Royal Capital came to be handed down as a prayer for safe travels.

Two hundred and eighty years had passed since its opening. Even as the roadway ground down by wheels was repaved time and again, and new stones were added to chipped railings, the 'Dragon's Back' had remained beloved by the people for its unchanging grandeur. Long after the people who built the bridge, their children, and their grandchildren had departed this world, the piled stones had faithfully fulfilled their role of continuing to carry the people of later generations to the opposite shore.

――That very Milesta Dragonback Bridge was, right now, about to collapse.

"――――"

Upon the bridge were roughly fifty dragon carriages.

From merchant carriages packed with cargo to shared transport carriages jammed with passengers, if one included the people on foot passing alongside them, the number of people crossing the bridge was well over a thousand.

All of them were frozen in their tracks by the sudden death agony raised by the Dragonback Bridge.

A massive fissure that appeared on the roadway without warning spread lightning-like cracks in an instant, ruthlessly swallowing the wheels of the dragon carriages lined up in procession and halting their advance. When the leading carriages stopped, those behind had no choice but to frantically haul on their reins. Ground dragons trying to avoid collisions pulled the carriages diagonally, the impact screeching against the iron railings securely enclosing the left and right sides of the bridge.

All the while, the sounds of the Dragonback Bridge breaking apart and the violent shaking struck at the hearts of those atop it, people pushing and shoving between the vehicles as chaos spiraled out of control.

Just as it seemed that all was lost—,

"――Icicle Line!"

A high, sonorous, and beautiful voice rang out, painting over the bridge's dying shriek with a different sound.

What echoed afresh was the fleeting resonance of freezing air cracking. It was the guide of absolute ice, sealing off the mouths to the abyss that had opened in the Dragonback Bridge alongside the tremors, forcibly stitching it together—,

"Geez! Doing something so awful just to look for us...!"

Amidst the mist-laden wind exposed to the chill, rolling grains of ice across the bridge, a beautiful girl whose long silver hair danced—Emilia—narrowed her almond-shaped eyes, her anger laid bare.

And then—,

"I won't let you! I'm reeally mad now!"

Holding out her hands, she sealed the massive fissures that were supposed to have caused the collapse of the Milesta Dragonback Bridge.

The method of freezing and sealing the wounds as if staunching a hemorrhage was utterly rough, but in this situation, it could be called the optimal solution for protecting over a thousand lives.

How reassuring her unhesitating decisiveness was in this kind of intuitive moment.

"Hey, anyone who can move, don't stand still! Leave the cargo behind! Your lives matter more!"

Taking advantage of that reassurance, Felt moved her own legs as well, leaping onto the roof of a tilted carriage and straining her voice while scanning the rear of the vehicle line.

The great fissure running through the center of the bridge had been sealed with ice, but that didn't mean carriages that had dropped their wheels could simply pull back out. Pressed axles snapped, and ground dragons dragged down by the sinking carriages neighed frantically. No matter how much the drivers whipped their reins from the coachboxes, it was a pitfall they couldn't recover from—,

"Dammit, if that's how it's gonna be, then I'll..."

"――Lady Felt, stay here."

Just before she bent her knees to leap, a low voice was left behind as a colossal weight was launched.

Leaving shoe prints on the bridge with his stomping feet, sprinting at a terrifying speed was a block of solid rock—no, Marcos. Reaching a carriage being swallowed by a hole, he grabbed the leather harness connecting the ground dragon and the vehicle, twisting and tearing it apart with inhuman strength.

The ground dragon, suddenly freed from the weight, stumbled forward, and Marcos's outstretched arm caught the heavily tilting carriage with one hand, yanking the wheel out of the hole and setting it back on the roadway.

Witnessing that monstrous strength, Felt caught her breath, but decided to leave that side to Marcos and train her eyes elsewhere—,

"――hk?!"

From the violent tremor that immediately followed, Felt was slammed against the roof of the carriage.

With a groan squeezed from the back of her throat and eyes forced open through tears, Felt looked around to see what had happened, only for a cold wind to blast across her skin. It was a wind carrying white spray, and the moment she realized the true nature of that spray was crushed grains of ice, she noticed it for the first time. ――Countless cracks had run like a spider's web through the white ice that had halted the bridge's collapse and served as their new lifeline.

"Wh-what..."

...happened? The murmur never fully took shape.

Faster than that, the chunks of ice gripping the railings crumbled, and the seams of ice sealing the roadway turned uniformly white and cloudy. A scream rose from the bridge, different from moments ago but leading to the exact same outcome; the collapse that should have been arrested resumed, and the Milesta Dragonback Bridge began to undulate.

As the resonance and shaking twisted Felt's insides—,

"...I'll shatter it. I shattered it."

Mingled with the screams of the bridge was a terribly flat voice.

Instinctively turning around, in Felt's field of vision, a slender figure stood upon a broken railing beyond the line of carriages. It was a gloomy-faced woman with waving green hair and a sickly pale complexion.

Standing motionless upon the railing, she wore a peculiar gauntlet on her left hand, and from the tips of her fingers, white grains spilled, scattering down into the Milesta Gorge from afar. ――They looked like grains of ice.

Without understanding what that meant, Felt was simply certain.

――That woman was the very culprit trying to smash the Milesta Dragonback Bridge to pieces.

"Maid! It's that woman!!"

Spurred by that conviction, Felt pointed at the green-haired woman and shouted.

Instantly, the one who moved in response was Ram, who was gripping the reins on the dragon carriage's driver's box. The moment she saw the target Felt was pointing at, she demanded no detailed explanation whatsoever.

Simply aiming the tip of the cane she swiftly drew at the opponent,

"El Fura!"

The unleashed blade of wind flew smoothly across the chaos-swept bridge, tracing a masterful trajectory.

An assault that would have produced numerous collateral victims on the way if cast by a second-rate mage instead aimed unerringly for the target woman, closing in on the enemy as if slipping through obstacles.

Just when it seemed the wind blade would mercilessly take off the woman's slender neck—the wind blade burst like the surface of struck water, scattering and vanishing.

"I shattered it."

Leaning her body atop the railing, the woman muttered as she rubbed her raised fingertips together.

It made no sense. But there was no time to demand an explanation. Her attack having been blocked by some method, Ram nevertheless braced herself to fire a second and third volley in succession.

However—,

"――Below! Someone look below!"

That frantic voice belonged to Filore, who had rolled down from the carriage.

Having leaped out of the carriage bed to rush to a boy crying on the roadway, Filore pointed beyond the crumbled railing, desperately calling out to those around her. Sweeping her gaze toward where that finger was pointing, Felt understood what Filore was screaming about.

Far, far below the bridge, upon the distant river surface, a massive white shadow was swaying.

"Ain't that a ship...?!"

At first, it was so massive she thought a mansion or something was drifting by.

It was a fairly luxurious ship, with two decks of cabins stacked one atop the other. Its open-air deck and window-lined passageways were packed full; it was clearly carrying a huge crowd.

The river mist hid most of it, but judging from what she could see, that ship must be carrying several hundred passengers too.

That ship was right now about to pass directly beneath the falling Dragonback Bridge.

"――No!!"

The shrill scream came from Emilia, who had spotted the same thing as Felt.

The reason she screamed was that the sheet of ice spread out to support the Dragonback Bridge cracked, peeling away along with part of the bridge piers supporting the structure, plummeting straight toward the ship crossing the river.

Just before the rock mass fused with ice could crush the ship from directly above, an iron railing thrown with fierce momentum pierced it diagonally, causing it to explode and scatter in midair.

"Rock-face...!"

The one who had done it was Marcos, who was rescuing people from an overturned carriage on the bridge.

Grabbing a broken section of railing and forcibly ripping it out, he had brandished the iron railing, which must have weighed several hundred kilograms, like a spear, accurately hurling it into the falling rock mass.

As a result, the boulder was smashed to pieces before striking the ship, sparing the vessel from sinking. However, the falling shattered debris must have caused injuries. Above all, the crisis didn't end there.

Both the transit of the ship below and the collapse of the Milesta Dragonback Bridge were still ongoing.

"――Big Sis Emilia!"

"I know!"

The continuous screams of the bridge shifted into a lower, heavier register.

Instinct sensed that this would lead to a fatal collapse. In fact, without any great tremor, feeling a strangely slow sinking sensation beneath her feet, Felt saw the entire bridge begin to tilt.

"――――"

Placing her hands on the ground, Emilia stitched the breaking bridge together with the white ice spreading from beneath her feet.

The way the white frost-fog produced by an immense amount of mana swelled explosively might have looked from the valley floor like a colossal flower of ice bursting through the dragon's back into bloom.

Aware that the lives of everyone on the bridge and aboard the ship crossing the river hung on that flower of ice, Felt spun on her heel and kicked off the air, using the shattered paving stones as footholds.

Stepping on the shoulders of the surrounding crowd and the rims of stranded carriages, she bounded over the convoy blocking the way, closing the distance to the green-haired woman who had triggered this catastrophe at tremendous speed.

Even now, the woman's raised hand gripped a glittering mass—a fragment of ice.

Inside Felt's hyper-focused head, a part of her brain exerting chillingly cold calculation urged her to slice off the woman's arm before those fingers could close.

Leaping past the final carriage, Felt swung back the dagger gripped in a reverse hold—it was right at that moment.

"――Huh?"

Suddenly, Felt's shadow vanished from the roadway. ――No, it hadn't vanished. A far greater shadow had fallen over it, depriving Felt's shadow of its place.

The darkness that crossed beneath her feet made her think of a cloud at first. Because it was simply that massive. But amidst the river mist swirling violently and the canopies of the carriages lining the bridge flapping in unison in the same direction, what Felt witnessed wasn't a cloud, but something possessing a far clearer, physical form.

"Wh... at?"

Looking up, Felt opened her mouth, entirely forgetting to lower the dagger she had swung back. Even the hostile woman had stopped her hand that was pinching the ice, looking up at the exact same thing as Felt.

Above the gorge, higher than the cliffs on either bank, as if looking down upon the dragon's back, there was a castle. ――Up in the sky, there was a castle.

"――A dream?"

No matter how you looked at it, Felt couldn't help but doubt her own eyes.

Yet, no matter how much she doubted it, there was unmistakably a castle right there. A fortress exposing the bottom that should have been rooted in the earth was floating, pushing aside the mist filling the valley.

While everyone caught their breath at that utterly off-the-charts spectacle—,

"――Subaru!!"

Alone among them, Emilia cried out, her gaze fixed not on the castle that had appeared, but on a single point upon it.

One could see the desperation from moments before clearing from Emilia's wide amethyst eyes, replaced by an expanding light of pure joy. Drawn by it, Felt looked as well. ――Upon a balcony jutting out toward them from the castle, two figures were standing side by side.

They were a girl in a dress, and a black-haired boy holding that girl in his arms—,

"Let 'em have it, Beako!!"

"――Ul Minya!!"

A spirited shout overlapped with a solemn incantation, and an amethyst radiance filled the sky.

Countless unleashed arrows of light poured down from the balcony, striking the Dragonback Bridge like rain. Passing over people's heads, slipping between the convoy, the light arrows plunged one after another into the tilted bridge girders and the white ice barely holding them together—and the bridge pierced by the light transformed into amethyst crystals.

"――――"

The tilting ceased, and a solid sensation returned beneath the soles of Felt's feet. It was proof that the bridge girders, which should have sunk without recourse, had dug in their heels at the very brink.

And a beat later, the people witnessed what had become of the Milesta Dragonback Bridge.

That was to say—,

"Sheesh, bro's as completely insane as ever."

Felt couldn't stop a half-exasperated, half-impressed voice from slipping out.

